 SAYS

A reader's supplement on the arcs main changed at Review 80 (agy-002's), its first two findings.

ONE, computed by main, holds. A bracket that main wrote on B1884's page at this review says that LR keeps
exactly one point at N = 5, and two at other levels. That is false. The lines of (Z/N)^2 that LR keeps are
the roots of x^2 - 3x + 1 modulo N. The sentence is main's, from its folder, and is written as main gives
it: one at N = 5; at any other level as many as x^2 - 3x + 1 has roots modulo N, which is none wherever 5 is
not a square; to level 60, none at 51 levels, one at 5, two at 11, 19, 29, 31, 41, 55 and 59.
It stands in a dated bracket after the two that were there. What was written is kept.

Held against it first, as the card asks: the note of this series' third part on the same place (173 levels
of 198 with none, 25 with two, four at 209). Its numbers agree with main's: main's seven levels to 60 are
among the 25.

The lock counts it by brute force, both ways: the lines LR keeps, by the arc's own function, are as many as
the roots of that quadratic at every level from 2 to 200; the counts to 60 are main's; no line is kept
wherever 5 is not a square; and at N = 2, where 5 is a square, none is kept either.

TWO, not checked by main, made again here. The page was corrected at Review 79's second round to say that
the arc is computed once. The script's own heading still said that its expectations were written before the
run. It has a dated note under it, in the page's words. And the lock held one of main's four mends of this
arc: it now holds the sentences of the other corrections of the page's first section as well.

The lock passes: 10 tests, and one that runs the script on request. Every gate passes but the count of
landings. The lock of B1862, whose tripwire reads this page, passes.
